question:
How do I prevent users from modifying resources they do not own?

I am writing a server where I have an entity StoreOwner that "owns" or has a @OneToMany relationship to a Store entity (one store owner has 1 to N stores). Each Store has Offer and Item, each also have a @OneToMany relationship with the store (one store has 1 to N offers and 1 to N items).
I am already using GWT Xsrf protection and a session ID that gets associated with the logged in user after each log-in (cookie).
There's one thing missing IMHO: what if a logged in user sends a delete request to the server with IDs of items that he does not own of a store he does not own either? At the moment, I am doing this in my StoreService:
// StoreService.java

@Transactional
public ItemDTO deleteItem(String sessionId, Long storeId, ItemDTO itemDto) {

    // sessionId is the cookie I have placed in my database
    // This way I want to ensure that I am only accessing a store
    // that is associated with the logged in store owner (the user basically)
    Store store = this.storeOwnerRepository.getStore(sessionId, storeId);

    Item item = ConvertDTO.convertItem(store, itemDto);

    // Check if the store ID that I got using the cookie is the
    // same ID as the store ID from the item that should be deleted
    if(item .getStore().getId() == store.getId()) {
        item = this.storeOwnerRepository.deleteItem(item);
    } else {
        // If this didn't work we have a potentially hostile user:
        throw new RuntimeException("Is somebody trying to delete items of a store he doesn't own?");
    }

    itemDto = ConvertEntity.convertItem(item);
    return itemDto;
}

It is the first time that I am trying to write a bigger server application and I want to prevent users from doing such things.
My question is twofold: [1] does what I am doing would really prevent a logged in user from smuggling the IDs of another store he does not own to my server? In addition, [2] can I simplify this a little bit?
My problem is that as the application grows one might - every now and then - forget this check
if(item .getStore().getId() == store.getId()) { /* .. */ }

Of course, I could move that into my StoreOwnerRepository, but do I have better options?

candidate_qwen3_32b:
Your current approach partially addresses the issue but can be improved. [1] The check `item.getStore().getId() == store.getId()` is valid **only if** `getStore(sessionId, storeId)` ensures the store belongs to the authenticated user. If `getStore()` correctly validates ownership (e.g., by querying the `StoreOwner` entity linked to the session), then this check prevents unauthorized deletions. However, this logic is fragile if `getStore()` is bypassed or misused. [2] To simplify and enforce consistency, integrate ownership validation into the repository or service layer. For example, in Spring, use `@PreAuthorize` with SpEL to check ownership:  
```java
@PreAuthorize("#storeId in @storeService.getAuthorizedStoreIdsForUser(authentication.name)")
public ItemDTO deleteItem(String sessionId, Long storeId, ItemDTO itemDto) { ... }
```  
Alternatively, fetch the `StoreOwner` via the session, then verify the `store` belongs to them before proceeding. This centralizes logic, reduces repetition, and leverages Spring

question:
Can exploit vulnerability if program started with gdb, but segfaults if started without gdb

I'm currently trying to exploit a simple program called basic_vuln.c
#include <stdio.h>;
int main(int argc, char** argv) {
  char buf[64];  
  strcpy(buf, argv[1]);  
}

I'm using xubuntu 12.10 with 3.5.0-17 Linux Kernel, ASLR turned off and compiled with the current version of tiny c compiler.
Now to the actual problem, I'm injecting a basic shellcode which spawns /bin/sh.
The problem is that when I execute it within gdb, everything works fine and I get a shell. But if I start the program within bash I get a segfault.
I tried to get a core dump to see where the problem lies, but I guess because of the corrupt memory I don't get any (if I don't give the program any arguments, it segfaults and I get a core dump).
Any ideas? If you require more details on the system like gdb version or similar, I'll post it as fast as I can after request.

candidate_llama_3_2_1b_instruct:
The issue is due to the way the program is using the `strcpy` function. The `strcpy` function copies the string from `argv[1]` to `buf`, but it does not null-terminate the string. This means that when you run the program with a string containing a null character, it will corrupt the memory after the null character, causing the program to segfault when it tries to access the memory after the null character.

To fix this, you need to add a null-terminator to the end of the string. You can do this by using the `strcpy_s` function, which is a safer alternative to `strcpy` that adds a null-terminator. Here's an updated version of the program:

```c
#include <stdio.h>;
#include <string.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main(int argc, char** argv) {
  char buf

question:
Proxy not rejecting illegal host header when coming from my VM provider's network

I have a django app hosted on a linode VM, proxied by nginx on that same VM.
In my nginx config I have a block to deny bad host header requests:
## Deny illegal Host headers
if ($host !~* ^(mydomain.com|www.mydomain.com)$ ) {
    return 444;
}

This blocks almost all the bad requests, but I'm still getting occasional requests from a random linode host (the app sends me an email due to this host not being in the ALLOWED_HOSTS whitelist). Why are those requests able to get past nginx?

candidate_llama_3_2_1b_instruct:
The issue is due to the fact that the `ALLOWED_HOSTS` directive in your Django settings is not being applied to the proxy configuration. When you use a proxy, the proxy's configuration is applied to the proxied URL, not the original URL. Therefore, the `ALLOWED_HOSTS` directive is not being applied to the proxied URL, allowing the host header to be accepted. To fix this, you can add the `ALLOWED_HOSTS` directive to the proxy configuration, like this:
```nginx
http {
    ...
    server {
        listen 80;
        server_name mydomain.com www.mydomain.com;
        ...
        proxy_pass http://your-django-app:8000;
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
        proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forward

